# routine — a walk on its own IS a routine
#
# The other half of the recorder's prefix rule, and the half that is easy to get backwards.
#
# A repositioning step (move, the climbs, the crossings, a door) never emits a routine WHERE IT
# HAPPENS: it extends the prefix that a later step will be built on, so the walk into the workshop is
# part of the trade routine rather than a routine of its own. But if the session ends with steps in
# the prefix that no emission has claimed, that prefix is emitted at the end — following a track
# somewhere and stopping there is a "go there" routine, not a leftover
# (see RoutineRecorder.EmitTrailingPath).
#
# So: one move, nothing else, and the visit records exactly one routine of one step. The complement —
# a prefix already consumed by a terminus does NOT emit a second, redundant routine — is asserted at
# the end of routine_record_replay.cli.
#
# Read after LEAVING, because that is when a session's trailing prefix is finalised. `inspect` answers
# `routines` with no narration in progress for exactly this reason.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --auto-dialogue --location-type test --location-id 0 --npc-static --start-area "Test Field" --period dawn --observe-only "Test Yard"

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60

# One successful move — a recordable, repositioning verb — and nothing else.
strategy succeed
goal move
advance 12 180
click keyword 0
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect-verb move
expect Moved to:

# The filter has done its job and must be dropped before the next phase is generated: it names the
# area we have just walked INTO, and you cannot observe the room you are standing in — so leaving it
# set makes the next phase fall back and fails the run on a narrowing flag that narrowed nothing.
observe none

# Two presses, and the first one is not the exit. A move ends on a node transition, so the footer
# reads CONTINUE: pressing it closes the segment and opens the next phase in the new area, whose
# observation preview then has to be drained before the footer becomes LEAVE. Pressing once and
# waiting for WorldView just sits on that preview box until the wait times out.
click button
wait 60
advance 12 180
click button
wait mode WorldView 90

# The walk, saved as its own routine. Asserted by verb id rather than by the routine's name, which
# is built from the destination's display name and is content.
inspect routines
expect-state routines verbs=[move]
quit
